{
  "image_name": "1bedfb5c405bfe3877397b35325ac63f.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how glial precursor cells mature into oligodendrocytes or astrocytes, and highlights key traits and signaling changes along each path. In the upper part, oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPCs) progress through increasingly branched premyelinating and promyelinating stages to become myelinating oligodendrocytes. Early OPCs are shown as proliferative and migratory, while differentiation is associated with repression of WNT and SHH signaling. Beneath this sequence, broad gene-expression bars indicate changing marker profiles, with some factors persisting across much of the lineage and others declining as cells mature.\n\nThe middle part focuses on NG2 cells, depicted as a proliferative, highly migratory population that can remain undifferentiated. These cells can generate myelinating oligodendrocytes, promoted by cues such as position, demyelination, and possibly neuronal activity, and this capacity appears to persist into adulthood. A second branch suggests NG2 cells may also produce protoplasmic astrocytes, although this route is marked with more uncertainty and seems restricted to a developmental window.\n\nThe bottom part outlines astrocyte development, from astrocyte precursors to pioneering and then mature astrocytes. This transition is linked to repression of NOTCH signaling and JAK-STAT3 signaling, alongside lineage-associated factors shown as sustained expression bands.",
